## 6. Testing Connectivity

### Test 1 – Ping the OPNsense WireGuard Interface

```bash
ping -c 4 10.10.10.1
```

This checks whether the CachyOS client can communicate with the OPNsense WireGuard interface.

![Split-Tunnel Ping Test](../screenshots/SplitTunnel-Ping.png)

### Test 2 – Verify Routing to the Home LAN

```bash
ip route get 192.168.1.1
```

The output should show that traffic destined for the home LAN is routed through `wg0`.

![Split-Tunnel Routing Test](../screenshots/SplitTunnel-Routing.png)

### Test 3 – Verify Internet Traffic Uses the Normal Connection

```bash
curl -4 https://ifconfig.me
```

When tested from an external network, the displayed public IPv4 address should match the external network's public address rather than the home connection.

This confirms that general IPv4 internet traffic is not being routed through the VPN.

## 7. Disconnecting the VPN

To stop the split-tunnel connection:

```bash
sudo wg-quick down wg0
```

This removes the WireGuard interface and its associated routes.

## 8. Results

The split-tunnel configuration was successfully tested from an external Wi-Fi network.

The CachyOS laptop established a WireGuard handshake with the OPNsense server and was able to communicate with devices on the home network.

The configuration allowed remote access to internal resources while keeping general internet traffic outside the VPN tunnel.

This demonstrated the practical use of WireGuard for secure remote network access.
